For a quantitative or binary human trait Y measured on a population, three estimands are commonly considered:

(i) Twin-based (broad-sense) heritability H², estimated from concordance/correlation differences between monozygotic and dizygotic twin pairs;

(ii) SNP-based (genotype) heritability h²_SNP, defined as the proportion of phenotypic variance attributable to additive effects of common single-nucleotide polymorphisms genotyped on standard arrays, typically estimated by methods such as variance-component (GREML) or LD-score regression;

(iii) The variance R² explained by a concrete polygenic risk score (PRS), i.e. a linear predictor Σ_j β̂_j X_j whose weights β̂_j are estimated from genome-wide association summary statistics on a training cohort and evaluated out-of-sample.

Empirically, for most complex traits one observes the strict inequalities

   R²(PRS) < h²_SNP < H².

The problem is to give a quantitative, mechanistic account of the two gaps H² − h²_SNP (the "still-missing" heritability) and h²_SNP − R²(PRS) (the "still-hidden" heritability). Specifically: determine to what extent each of the following candidate sources contributes to these gaps, and whether they together suffice to close them:

(a) additive contributions of rare and low-frequency variants and structural variants not tagged by common SNP arrays;
(b) non-additive genetic effects (dominance, epistasis, gene–gene interactions);
(c) gene–environment interactions and gene–environment correlation;
(d) residual population structure and assortative mating biasing variance-component estimators;
(e) environmental confounding and shared-environment biases inflating twin-based estimates;
(f) indirect genetic effects from relatives (genetic nurture);
(g) limitations of the prediction model class (e.g. sparsity, linearity, training sample size) used to construct the PRS;
(h) the possibility that "heritability," as classically defined under the additive polygenic model, is itself misinterpreted or ill-posed for the traits in question.

Provide either a decomposition theorem/empirical accounting that closes the inequalities, or rigorous lower bounds showing which of (a)–(h) are individually necessary or sufficient.

